## The helper archive in msil-ld's undefined-symbol report (ImplementationPlan.md
## 5.14.2, 5.14.3 A1, A7 and A9; M25.d (1)). A report of an undefined name of
## class N1, N2 or N3 has one more line after its first line and before its
## '>>> referenced by' lines (M24; never-bind.test pins them beside the
## never-bind checks):
## - N1, a compiler runtime helper: a '__' name of
##   lto::LTO::getRuntimeLibcallSymbols (here __extendhfsf2) and, from M25,
##   the C complex helpers that clang calls by name and RuntimeLibcalls lacks
##   (__mulhc3, __mulsc3, __muldc3, __mulxc3, __multc3, __divhc3, __divsc3,
##   __divdc3, __divxc3 and __divtc3):
##     >>> a compiler runtime helper: link the toolchain's helper archive clang_rt.builtins
##   While an input archive named clang_rt.builtins.lib or
##   libclang_rt.builtins.a takes part (A1's names: windows-msvc's, and the
##   other triples', x86_64 MinGW included), whether or not a member of it is
##   extracted, the line reads instead
##     >>> a compiler runtime helper that the toolchain's helper archive clang_rt.builtins does not define
##   An archive of another name (libhelp.a, help.lib) keeps the first form.
## - N2, __atomic_load_4: on Linux triples
##     >>> an atomic operation: link with -latomic, as a native build does
##   and on Windows triples
##     >>> an atomic operation (libatomic's interface)
## - N3, __msil_example (no trailer definition offers it):
##     >>> a toolchain helper: link the toolchain's helper archive clang_rt.builtins; if it is linked, the object needs a newer toolchain
##   with or without the archive.
## - Any other '__' name, __start_x, has no line.
## Each case runs on both default triples through CIL objects, an LTO image
## and --lto-emit-asm text. A direct call is a bitcode reference, reported
## with the bitcode file. Links that extract a stand-in member have no text
## run: --lto-emit-asm resolves the bitcode's own references before code
## generation, as an image link does (A9), so with the archive on the line
## msil-ld extracts the member, a CIL object, and stops with M20's error,
## which one case pins on both triples; without the archive the call gets
## N1's report. The stand-in members are hidden, as -fvisibility=hidden
## compiles the archive's (A2).
## Also:
## - References that code generation creates after LTO ('<output>.lto.o'):
##   atomicrmw's __atomic_fetch_add_4 and the fpext of half's __extendhfsf2
##   get the same lines. In --lto-emit-asm text, which links no CIL object,
##   they are the backend's errors (A9; T-B for an '__atomic_' name):
##     runtime helper '<name>' needs the helper archive clang_rt.builtins, which only msil-ld links
##     runtime helper '<name>' is an atomic operation that only msil-ld links
## - A9 with the archive: --lto-emit-asm of a bitcode call of __divdc3, with
##   the archive at A1's path whose member defines it, prints only
##     <archive>(<member>): --lto-emit-asm cannot link CIL objects; link without --lto-emit-asm
##   and writes nothing; the image link of the same inputs succeeds.
## - The complex names never bind natively (5.14.2 N-2, M24's guard): all
##   ten stay undefined, each with N1's line, against a libgcc_s.so.1
##   stand-in on Linux and a libgcc_s_seh-1.dll import library on
##   windows-msvc that export them.
## - A call of a complex helper whose signature differs from the definition
##   of a stand-in member or of a bitcode input is N-1's error, never 5.9.8's
##   adapter and warning, and nothing is written:
##     function signature mismatch: <name> (a toolchain or compiler runtime helper)
##     >>> defined as <IL signature> in <file>
##     >>> called as <IL signature> in <file>
##   without the ' in <file>' parts when both are bitcode (5.9.8's LTO path).
## - A7: a -shared library that extracts hidden members defines them in
##   '<Module>' and exports neither. A program linked against it cannot use
##   them: its own reference stays undefined, and with the archive it links
##   its own copy.

# REQUIRES: llvm-cil-dump
# RUN: rm -rf %t && split-file %s %t && cd %t
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj lnx.ll -o lnx.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj win.ll -o win.o
# RUN: llvm-as lnx.ll -o lnx.bc
# RUN: llvm-as win.ll -o win.bc
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj ext-lnx.ll -o ext-lnx.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj ext-win.ll -o ext-win.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj div-lnx.ll -o div-lnx.o
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj div-win.ll -o div-win.o
# RUN: mkdir -p res/lib/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil res/lib/x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil
# RUN: llvm-ar rcs res/lib/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil/libclang_rt.builtins.a div-lnx.o
# RUN: llvm-ar rcs res/lib/x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil/clang_rt.builtins.lib div-win.o
# RUN: llvm-ar rcs libclang_rt.builtins.a ext-lnx.o
# RUN: llvm-ar rcs clang_rt.builtins.lib ext-win.o
# RUN: llvm-ar rcs libhelp.a ext-lnx.o
# RUN: llvm-ar rcs help.lib ext-win.o

## A1's paths under a stand-in resource directory, as the driver passes them.
# DEFINE: %{rt-lnx} = res/lib/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil/libclang_rt.builtins.a
# DEFINE: %{rt-win} = res/lib/x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil/clang_rt.builtins.lib

## The five reports, in the order of main's calls, with every line pinned
## (RPT). HF is __extendhfsf2's report, which a link that extracts its member
## does not print; HF-LINK and N1-LINK give __extendhfsf2's and __muldc3's
## N1 line its first form, HF-NODEF and N1-NODEF the "does not define" form;
## N2-LNX and N2-WIN are N2's line.
# DEFINE: %{link-lnx} = FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=RPT,HF,HF-LINK,N1-LINK,N2-LNX
# DEFINE: %{link-win} = FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=RPT,HF,HF-LINK,N1-LINK,N2-WIN
# DEFINE: %{nodef-lnx} = FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=RPT,HF,HF-NODEF,N1-NODEF,N2-LNX
# DEFINE: %{nodef-win} = FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=RPT,HF,HF-NODEF,N1-NODEF,N2-WIN
# DEFINE: %{four-link-lnx} = FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=RPT,N1-LINK,N2-LNX
# DEFINE: %{four-link-win} = FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=RPT,N1-LINK,N2-WIN
# DEFINE: %{four-nodef-lnx} = FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=RPT,N1-NODEF,N2-LNX
# DEFINE: %{four-nodef-win} = FileCheck %s --check-prefixes=RPT,N1-NODEF,N2-WIN

# RPT-NOT:        {{.}}
# HF:             {{^}}msil-ld: error: undefined symbol: __extendhfsf2{{$}}
# HF-LINK-NEXT:   {{^}}>>> a compiler runtime helper: link the toolchain's helper archive clang_rt.builtins{{$}}
# HF-NODEF-NEXT:  {{^}}>>> a compiler runtime helper that the toolchain's helper archive clang_rt.builtins does not define{{$}}
# HF-NEXT:        {{^}}>>> referenced by [[REF]]:(main){{$}}
# HF-EMPTY:
# HF-NOT:         {{.}}
# RPT:            {{^}}msil-ld: error: undefined symbol: __muldc3{{$}}
# N1-LINK-NEXT:   {{^}}>>> a compiler runtime helper: link the toolchain's helper archive clang_rt.builtins{{$}}
# N1-NODEF-NEXT:  {{^}}>>> a compiler runtime helper that the toolchain's helper archive clang_rt.builtins does not define{{$}}
# RPT-NEXT:       {{^}}>>> referenced by [[REF]]:(main){{$}}
# RPT-EMPTY:
# RPT-NEXT:       {{^}}msil-ld: error: undefined symbol: __atomic_load_4{{$}}
# N2-LNX-NEXT:    {{^}}>>> an atomic operation: link with -latomic, as a native build does{{$}}
# N2-WIN-NEXT:    {{^}}>>> an atomic operation (libatomic's interface){{$}}
# RPT-NEXT:       {{^}}>>> referenced by [[REF]]:(main){{$}}
# RPT-EMPTY:
# RPT-NEXT:       {{^}}msil-ld: error: undefined symbol: __msil_example{{$}}
# RPT-NEXT:       {{^}}>>> a toolchain helper: link the toolchain's helper archive clang_rt.builtins; if it is linked, the object needs a newer toolchain{{$}}
# RPT-NEXT:       {{^}}>>> referenced by [[REF]]:(main){{$}}
# RPT-EMPTY:
# RPT-NEXT:       {{^}}msil-ld: error: undefined symbol: __start_x{{$}}
# RPT-NEXT:       {{^}}>>> referenced by [[REF]]:(main){{$}}
# RPT-NOT:        {{.}}

